   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to terminal rental adjustment clauses\nin motor vehicle leases",
    "basePrintNo": "S3926",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3926-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1111, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3926\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to terminal rental adjustment\nclauses in motor vehicle leases\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nTo clarify how sales taxes must be calculated and remitted under termi-\nnal rental adjustment lease agreements.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would clarify the Tax Law to state that under\nvehicle leases containing a terminal rental adjustment clause, motor\nvehicle leasing companies have the flexibility to pay sales taxes on its\npurchases of fleet vehicles either up-front on the receipts of the sale\nat the time it purchases motor vehicles for lease to New York businesses\nor up-front on the lease consideration that the lessors receive from the\nlease of these vehicles for the first 32 months of the lease term and\nsubsequently on each renewal option thereafter.\n\nSection 2 of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCommercial motor vehicle lessors own and manage millions of vehicles\nwhich are leased to corporations, small businesses, nonprofit organiza-\ntions and government entities in all 50 states. These vehicles range\nfrom passenger cars to cargo vans and trucks that are customized and\noutfitted to fit business purposes, from electrical and plumbing repair\nand telecommunications installation, to wholesale food and beverage\ndistribution and fuel delivery. These vehicles are on the road every day\nand are part of the essential infrastructure of New York State's econo-\nmy.\n\nCommercial motor vehicle leases, which are also known as \"TRAC leases\",\nfeature a minimum term of approximately one year and monthly renewals\nthereafter until the customer returns the vehicle. The actual value of\nthe vehicle at the end of the lease term is then determined, usually by\nsale at wholesale auction. Each lease agreement features a Terminal\nRental Adjustment Clause (TRAC) that provides for a payment adjustment\nupon lease termination. If the actual value of the vehicle upon lease\ntermination is less than the value estimated in the lease agreement,\nthen the total lease computation may result in the customer having to\npay an additional lease payment. Conversely, if the value of the vehicle\nupon lease termination is greater than the estimated value, then the\ncustomer receives a refund of overpaid lease consideration throughout\nthe lease term.\n\nLessors of motor vehicles subject to TRAC leases struggle with how to\ncorrectly collect, remit and report New York state and local sales taxes\non these unique transactions. Unlike consumer leases with individual\nconsumers, TRAC leases are not for a fixed term and the amount of rent\nvaries based on a variety of factors. Many TRAC lease payments contain a\nvariable interest rate component whereby the rent is allowed to change\nas an underlying index interest rate fluctuates due to changing economic\nconditions. The vehicle is sold at the end of the lease and, pursuant to\nthe TRAC clause in the lease agreement, additional rent is paid by or\nrent previously paid is refunded to the lessee. Existing law is unclear\nas to how these factors increase or decrease the taxable lease consider-\nation.\n\nProviding commercial fleet lessors the option to pay tax on its\npurchases of its motor vehicles for lease would simplify tax compliance\nsince tax could be paid instead on the purchase price paid by the lessor\nto the seller as reflected on a bill of sale. This is the same manner by\nwhich sales and use tax is computed on any other motor vehicle purchased\nby an individual or business.  Allowing commercial fleet lessors to pay\nsales and use tax on its purchases of motor vehicles for lease would\nfurther accelerate tax revenues. If the up front tax election is made by\nthe lessor, tax would apply to the total vehicle purchase price paid by\nthe lessor rather than only the lease consideration paid by the lessee\nfor the use and possession of that vehicle. Moreover, tax paid on the\nlessor's purchase would be paid immediately to the seller at the time of\nsale (or, if the seller is not in New York State, to the Department of\nMotor Vehicles at the time of titling and registration) rather than over\nthe lease term.\n\nUnder current law, sales tax is paid up-front on the lease consideration\nreceived from the lease of these vehicles for the first 32 months of the\nlease term and subsequently on each renewal option thereafter. If this\nlegislation is enacted, this same treatment will continue, except that\ncommercial fleet lessors will have the additional option of paying all\nof the sales and use tax due on its purchase up-front. This will accel-\nerate the tax paid to New York State and the tax will be paid on a high-\ner tax base (subject to any reduction to the taxable sales price such as\nrebates, incentives and trade-ins).\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-2020: Referred To Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nEnactment of this legislation would provide a new sales tax payment\noption for fleet vehicle leasing companies that would accelerate\npayments by paying upfront on the vehicle purchase price and it would\nalso enable NYS to receive additional revenues. The amount of additional\nrevenues would depend on how many companies opted in to this new option.\nBased on recent fleet vehicle industry figures, the estimated range of\nadditional annual revenue would be $600,000 to $1.6 million paid to NYS.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
